conazole and other azoles are associated with fetal malformations\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Systemic use of itraconazole during pregnancy is strongly discouraged due to its association with fetal malformations. Guidelines explicitly list itraconazole, along with fluconazole, voriconazole, and ketoconazole, as contraindicated for systemic use in pregnant women. Although human data are limited, animal studies and clinical caution support this restriction to prevent teratogenic effects [Data: Relationships (8774)]. This positions azole antifungals as high-risk agents in reproductive-age females unless benefits clearly outweigh risks.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Complex interactions between antiretroviral and antifungal therapies in HIV/AIDS patients\",\n            \"explanation\": \"In HIV/AIDS patients, the combination of antiretroviral drugs and azole antifungals presents substantial drug interaction challenges. Both drug classes are metabolized by CYP450 enzymes and often act as inhibitors or inducers, leading to altered plasma concentrations that can compromise efficacy or increase toxicity. Enhanced antiretroviral regimens (‘boosted’ ART) are particularly prone to contraindicated interactions with azoles, requiring vigilant management [Data: Relationships (21542, 21541, 34463)]. This complexity is especially relevant given the high prevalence of opportunistic fungal infections in this immunocompromised population.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Fluconazole interacts with hormonal contraceptives, potentially reducing efficacy\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Although direct evidence for fluconazole’s impact on oral contraceptives is less robust than for some antibiotics, its inclusion in interaction analyses alongside other antimicrobials suggests potential concerns. Certain antimicrobials reduce contraceptive efficacy by altering gut flora and interrupting enterohepatic recirculation of hormones. While fluconazole’s specific effect on gestodene (a progestin in some contraceptives) is noted in interaction databases, the clinical significance remains nuanced [Data: Relationships (36485)]. Nonetheless, caution and backup contraception may be warranted during concomitant use.\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 8.2,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The community poses high clinical impact due to multiple severe, well-documented drug-drug interactions involving widely used antifungal agents that can lead to life-threatening toxicities, treatment failure, or fetal harm.\"\n}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null, "provider_specific_fields": {"refusal": null}}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "created": 1782126887, "model": "qwen3-max", "object": "chat.completion", "moderation": null, "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1318, "prompt_tokens": 6985, "total_tokens": 8303, "completion_tokens_details": null, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 1280}}, "formatted_response": null, "content": "{\n    \"title\": \"Fluconazole and Azole Antifungal Drug Interaction Network\",\n    \"summary\": \"This community centers on fluconazole and other azole antifungal agents (e.g., itraconazole, miconazole, clotrimazole) and their extensive pharmacological interactions with various drugs, patient populations, and metabolic pathways. Key relationships include contraindications in tuberculosis patients, elevation of immunosuppressant and anticoagulant levels, inhibition of CYP enzymes (particularly CYP2C19 and CYP3A4), and associations with serious adverse effects like fetal malformations. The network highlights significant clinical risks arising from drug-drug interactions (DDIs), especially in vulnerable populations such as pregnant women, HIV/AIDS patients, and transplant recipients.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summa